# assets/source/maps

`slice-sea.tmj` is the slice sea (§3, §18 stage 2), a Tiled JSON map of 111 × 111 tiles. It uses only the subset documented in `packages/content/src/tiledMap.ts`:

- orthogonal, finite, 24 × 24 px tiles (the size the game draws them);
- one embedded tileset (`firstgid` 1) whose tiles have a `type`: `sea`, `land`, `shoal` or `dock`. The art is placeholder colours drawn by the game, so the tileset has no image;
- a tile layer named `terrain`, stored as a plain array of tile ids, every cell filled;
- an object layer named `objects` with an `island` rectangle per island (on tile boundaries, named, string property `theme`: `tropical`, `volcanic`, `ruin` or `mist`), a `spawn` point per spawn slot (int property `slot` from 0, string property `facing`: `E`, `SE`, `S`, `SW`, `W`, `NW`, `N` or `NE`), one `hub` point without properties: the crossing near the centre that every route between islands passes, and a `final-socket` rectangle per hidden final-island socket (§3: on tile boundaries, covering a dockless islet, int properties `claimX` and `claimY` naming its claim tile, the sea tile beside it whose centre is the §5 claim point).

The map is generated: `sliceSeaSketch.ts` draws its north-west quarter in ASCII (`.` sea, `#` land, `%` shoal, `D` dock) and mirrors it into the other three, with the islands, spawn slots and hub. Shoal walls on the midlines open only near the hub, so ships sailing between islands meet there; reefs keep every stretch of open water within sight of an obstacle. The row three tiles south of the hub stays clear for 20 tiles either side of it, for the tests. One islet north of Palm Key, mirrored into four, gives the final-island sockets: two in the north-centre sector, two in the south-centre one, all alike, so neither the map nor a revealed sector says which one a match picks; the server picks it with `chooseFinalSocket` from a secret it draws, never the public match seed. `npm run map:build -- --from-sketch` writes it, and a unit test fails while the committed `.tmj` differs from what the sketch draws. To change the map, edit the sketch and run, from the repository root:

```
npm run map:build -- --from-sketch
```

`npm run map:build` (with or without `--from-sketch`) refuses a map outside the subset or one that fails the map validators (four islands with docks, shoals, reachable docks and slots, travel times, no open water beyond the sight radius from land, a shoal or a dock, and every route between islands through the hub; for the sockets, claim tiles within 90 s of every spawn, two sockets to a sector, 12 navigable tiles in each treasure circle, and 8 tiles' clearance from the hub, spawns, docks and the test routes), prints the travel times, the largest distance to an obstacle, the open-sea share and each route's closest approach to the hub, and each socket's sector, worst time from a spawn and navigable tiles in its circle, and writes `packages/content/src/maps/sliceSea.ts`. A unit test fails while that module is out of date.
